WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.512
Many teams inherit older C++ code with raw buffers, C-style strings, custom allocation, and unclear ownership.

2
00:00:06.512 --> 00:00:10.343
Prevention does not require a complete rewrite on day one.

3
00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:17.238
It requires a plan that improves the highest-risk areas without destabilizing behavior the product still depends on.

4
00:00:17.238 --> 00:00:19.920
Prioritize externally reachable and parser-heavy paths.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.581
File importers, protocol handlers, serialization code, string formatting, command processing, and boundary-heavy glue code are good places to start

6
00:00:27.581 --> 00:00:32.561
because they often receive variable-length data and translate it into internal state.

7
00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:35.242
Wrap risky legacy APIs behind safer interfaces.

8
00:00:35.242 --> 00:00:40.222
A wrapper can centralize length validation, capacity checks, encoding rules, and error behavior.

9
00:00:40.222 --> 00:00:45.968
It can also give new code a better contract while older internals are refactored gradually.

10
00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:47.884
Replace manual memory management incrementally.

11
00:00:47.884 --> 00:00:51.331
A raw dynamic array may become a `std::vector`.

12
00:00:51.331 --> 00:00:55.928
A pointer and length pair may become a span at the boundary.

13
00:00:55.928 --> 00:01:01.674
A custom string buffer may become a string or a small domain-specific buffer object.

14
00:01:01.674 --> 00:01:05.122
Each change should have a clear behavior-preservation goal.

15
00:01:05.122 --> 00:01:08.187
Regression tests are essential before changing buffer code.

16
00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:14.699
Tests should capture intended behavior for normal input, boundary sizes, truncation policy, failure paths, and malformed data.

17
00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:20.445
When tests exist, refactoring becomes a controlled engineering activity instead of a leap of faith.

18
00:01:20.445 --> 00:01:21.594
Document remaining risk.

19
00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:28.489
If a legacy interface cannot be changed yet, record its contract, validation expectations, owners, and planned replacement path.

20
00:01:28.489 --> 00:01:32.320
Incremental improvement works best when the remaining work is visible.
